Lab: Deploy Managed and Networked Applications on Kubernetes
Objective
Deploy a MySQL database and a PHP web application, connect them together, and expose the web app externally.
You will learn how to:
•	Deploy a MySQL database container
•	Deploy a PHP web application
•	Configure environment variables
•	Create services for internal communication
•	Expose an application using a route
________________________________________
1. Start the Lab Environment
lab start deploy-review
This command:
•	Prepares the cluster
•	Creates the project database-applications
________________________________________
2. Login to OpenShift
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
________________________________________
3. Switch to the Project
oc project database-applications
________________________________________
4. Deploy the MySQL Database
Create the MySQL deployment.
oc create deployment mysql-app \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/mysql-app:v1
Verify pod status:
oc get pods
Example output:
mysql-app-75dfd58f99-5xfqc   0/1   Error
The pod is failing.
________________________________________
5. Identify the Root Cause
Check logs.
oc logs <mysql-pod-name>
Example:
You must specify the following environment variables:
MYSQL_USER
MYSQL_PASSWORD
MYSQL_DATABASE
The container requires environment variables to start.
________________________________________
6. Configure Required Environment Variables
Set the variables for the deployment.
oc set env deployment/mysql-app \
MYSQL_USER=redhat \
MYSQL_PASSWORD=redhat123 \
MYSQL_DATABASE=world_x
Verify pods again:
oc get pods
Expected:
mysql-app-xxxx   1/1   Running
________________________________________
7. Load the Database Data
Run the SQL script inside the pod.
oc exec -it <mysql-pod-name> \
-- /bin/bash -c "mysql -uredhat -predhat123 </tmp/world_x.sql"
________________________________________
8. Verify Database Access
Open a shell inside the container.
oc rsh <mysql-pod-name>
Connect to MySQL:
mysql -uredhat -predhat123 world_x
Expected:
Welcome to the MySQL monitor
Exit MySQL:
exit
Exit container:
exit
________________________________________
9. Create a Service for MySQL
Expose the MySQL deployment.
oc expose deployment mysql-app \
--name mysql-service \
--port 3306 \
--target-port 3306
Verify service:
oc get services
Example:
mysql-service   ClusterIP   172.30.x.x   3306/TCP
Check endpoints:
oc get endpoints
Example:
mysql-service   10.8.0.102:3306
________________________________________
10. Deploy the PHP Web Application
Create the PHP deployment.
oc create deployment php-app \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/php-webapp:v1
Verify pods:
oc get pods
Expected:
php-app-xxxx     Running
mysql-app-xxxx   Running
________________________________________
11. Create a Service for the PHP Application
Expose the deployment.
oc expose deployment php-app \
--name php-svc \
--port 8080 \
--target-port 8080
Verify:
oc get services
Example:
php-svc   ClusterIP   172.30.x.x   8080/TCP
Check endpoints:
oc get endpoints
Example:
php-svc   10.8.0.107:8080
________________________________________
12. Expose the Web Application Externally
Create a route.
oc expose service/php-svc --name phpapp
Verify route:
oc get routes
Example:
phpapp   phpapp-database-applications.apps.ocp4.example.com
________________________________________
13. Test the Application
Open the route in a browser:
phpapp-database-applications.apps.ocp4.example.com
The PHP application should retrieve data from the MySQL database.
________________________________________
14. Grade the Lab
lab grade deploy-review
Fix any errors if needed.
________________________________________
15. Finish the Lab
lab finish deploy-review
________________________________________
